 want to  get a bit of a sense of your sense of  spending priorities and i understand  there is only so much you can tell us  ahead of uh next week  fuel duty is an area where there's been  lots of speculation about should people  feel that there is action that may be  taken there yeah and i know it's  frustrating for you for people watching  obviously i can't comment on specific  things but what i would say is i  understand that i have a rural  constituency people are incredibly  reliant on their cars and this is one of  the biggest bills that people face or  watching it go up right and we're all  seeing that and when we're filling up  our cards i get that that's why we've  froze in fuel duty already i announced  that in autumn it's the 11th  consecutive year of fuel duty freezes  and that really helps people i know that  and of course fuel duty energy these are  things that people on lower incomes  spend a disproportionate amount of their  income on and it hits them particularly  hard when the bills go up and things are  about to get worse for them as well of  course because in april benefits are  going up just three percent inflation  we're expected to be around eight  percent can you at least say  that you're going to be targeting  for your help that you can give the  people who are going to need it the most  yeah that's that's what we have done  over the past couple of years  i'm asking i'm trying to throw forward  yeah and of course you can always say  judge me by my actions and over the last  couple of years you'll see and all the  research that's published shows this  that those on the lowest incomes have  have actually seen the greatest amount  of support so there will be action you  think targeted towards particularly  those on low income so actually if you  look at what we've already done and  energy you mentioned energy which is a  good one i i i i i i acknowledge you i  want to talk about your record but i'm  just aware of timings well energy this  is something about this is something  that's about to come in right and there  are lots of different ways that you  could help people with energy bills and  you're going to have racial weaves on  later so one thing that the labour party  said is that we should just cut vat on  on energy that is the opposite of  targeted and it would mean someone like  you or me who probably has a larger than  average energy bill would get a larger  discount well actually what we've done  is provide 150 pounds to 80  of british households four out of five  english households that is obviously  going to mean a lot more to someone on  low incomes as you said it's much more  targeted support and it will get there  much quickly so we're doing exactly as  you have said we've um talked about how  difficult things are going to get and  that you can only mitigate so much of  that  but of course this is the time you are  choosing to go ahead with tax risers in  the form of national insurance i think  it's been um it's pretty fair to say  that those aren't going to be going  ahead if you're not going to scrap it  should we expect to see at least some  movement on the thresholds at which  people start paying these taxes i think  you know take a step back why why are we  introducing a new health and nhs and  care levy it's because i care deeply  about the nhs and i know that the  country does as well we were faced with  a situation where millions of people  were going to be waiting years to get  the treatment they wanted as we  recovered from chronovirus  i'll get this  we didn't think that was acceptable for  anybody and we wanted to address it and  the team at the nhs are prepared to work  incredibly hard to work through that  backlog i wanted to make sure that they  had the funding that they need and  deserve in order to to do that and  that's why we're introducing this levy  people can be reassured every penny of  it unlike all other taxes every penny of  this tax goes straight to the thing they